{\looseness=-1 \textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} Frozen RR-MoA beats \emph{full fine-tuning} (all 8 blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 LRs) by $12$--$79\%$ on all 6 LTSF datasets (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:frozen_paradox}). \emph{This headline gap is a two-factor contrast} (frozen+MoE vs.\ unfrozen+single-head) that decomposes into two mechanisms with very different effect sizes:
\emph{(M1) MoE architectural advantage (dominant, $26$--$79\%$).} The frozen five-expert mixture with learned per-sample routing provides an architectural advantage that a single unfrozen head cannot match even with full parameter access: per-sample expert selection captures input-regime structure that no single topology in our pool represents on its own (the best-fixed-adapter row of Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa} loses by $26$--$79\%$ \emph{at the same freeze level}, isolating M1 from M2).
\emph{(M2) Within-architecture freeze advantage (smaller, $\leq 13\%$, non-unidirectional).} Within RR-MoA itself (architecture held fixed), frozen is best on $4/6$ datasets; light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$ on the other two. The within-architecture freeze effect is therefore real but bounded and direction-mixed; the headline $12$--$79\%$ number is dominated by M1.
\emph{Causal evidence for M2 specifically.} Unfreezing triggers gradient co-adaptation (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}): the dominant expert reshapes the shared backbone, collapsing routing, and freezing prevents this loop and preserves expert diversity. The Router-Detached Gradient Flow ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}) unfreezes the backbone but cuts the routing-weighted gradient feedback, degrading MSE by $+35$--$100\%$ on three datasets — a direct counterfactual on the co-adaptation term. Disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers full-FT performance by $54$--$61\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), pinning the mechanism on instance normalization rather than optimization. SR-MoA (a different router architecture) independently replicates the within-architecture pattern (frozen best on $4/6$; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Even after $90$-config hyperparameter tuning (learning rates $10^{-5}$ to $10^{-6}$, $100$ epochs, cosine schedule with $10$-epoch warmup, layerwise LR decay, weight decay sweep, gradient accumulation up to effective batch $512$), full fine-tuning still loses by $51$--$71\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), ruling out optimization confounds.}

\begin{proposition}[Gradient Co-Adaptation Drives Entropy Collapse]
\label{prop:frozen}
Consider a $2$-expert MoE with backbone $\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$, router $p{=}\sigma(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{h})$, loss $\ell{=}p\,\ell_1{+}(1{-}p)\,\ell_2$. Under gradient descent on $(A,\mathbf{w})$: \textup{(i)} the dominant expert's loss decreases faster ($\dot{\ell}_k \propto {-}p_k\|\nabla_\mathbf{h}\ell_k\|^2$), and $\dot{p}$ amplifies whichever expert currently has lower loss, creating self-reinforcing collapse toward $p{\in}\{0,1\}$; \textup{(ii)} freezing $A$ removes the co-adaptation feedback through the backbone (full non-collapse additionally requires $(\ell_1{-}\ell_2)$ to flip sign across input regimes, i.e., expert specialization, which is satisfied by the topologically distinct RR-MoA pool). Proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
\end{proposition}

