\vspace{-1.2em}
\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
\vspace{-0.6em}

MoE adapters on instance-normalized TSFMs fail for structural reasons: the normalization needed for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics a downstream router needs. Raw pre-normalization routing fixes the failure and motivates Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}; $R(\mathcal{D})$ identifies where it applies. More broadly, when a foundation model's input pipeline destroys signal a downstream router needs, optimization-side rescues (load balancing, z-loss, ReLU routing) cannot recover what is structurally absent — the fix is restoring the input, not retuning the optimizer. The same mechanism appears in a vision control (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}); generalization to untested normalization families is bounded by Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}.

\paragraph{Limitations.} \emph{(i)~Normalization specificity:} our diagnosis applies to instance-level normalization (RevIN, BatchNorm, GroupNorm); attention-based or learned normalizers are uncharacterized. \emph{(ii)~Signal-ratio boundary cases:} $R(\mathcal{D})$ is a single monotone proxy and is not sharp on datasets with strong shape-statistics coupling (Solar, $R{=}0.06$). \emph{(iii)~Tasks:} forecasting and imputation only; anomaly detection, classification, and irregularly-sampled tasks are deferred.

